source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:2://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:3://! Up until MIR building, this attribute has absolutely no effect. The `mir!` macro is a normal
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:4://! decl macro that expands like any other, and the code goes through parsing, name resolution and
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:5://! type checking like all other code. In MIR building we finally detect whether this attribute is
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:6://! present, and if so we branch off into this module, which implements the attribute by
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:7://! implementing a custom lowering from THIR to MIR.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:8://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:9://! The result of this lowering is returned "normally" from `build_mir`, with the only
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:10://! notable difference being that the `injected` field in the body is set. Various components of the
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:11://! MIR pipeline, like borrowck and the pass manager will then consult this field (via
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:12://! `body.should_skip()`) to skip the parts of the MIR pipeline that precede the MIR phase the user
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:13://! specified.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:14://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:15://! This file defines the general framework for the custom parsing. The parsing for all the
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:16://! "top-level" constructs can be found in the `parse` submodule, while the parsing for statements,
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/custom/mod.rs:17://! terminators, and everything below can be found in the `parse::instruction` submodule.
