source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:2://! "Go to file" feature to silently ignore all files in the module, probably
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:3://! because it assumes that "build" is a build-output directory.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:4://! See <https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/134365>.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:5://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:6://! ## The `let this = self;` idiom (LET_THIS_SELF)
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:7://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:8://! Throughout MIR building there are several places where a `Builder` method
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:9://! needs to borrow `self`, and then re-expose it to a closure as `|this|`.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:10://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:11://! In complex builder methods, potentially with multiple levels of nesting, it
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:12://! would thus become necessary to mentally keep track of whether the builder
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:13://! is `self` (at the top level) or `this` (nested in a closure), or to replace
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:14://! one with the other when moving code in or out of a closure.
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:15://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:16://! (The borrow checker will prevent incorrect usage, but having to go back and
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:17://! satisfy the borrow checker still creates contributor friction.)
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:18://!
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:19://! To reduce that friction, some builder methods therefore start with
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:20://! `let this = self;` or similar, allowing subsequent code to uniformly refer
source/rustc_mir_build/src/builder/mod.rs:21://! to the builder as `this` (and never `self`), even when not nested.
