codex
The real counterexample is a bare erased `!` expression used as a block statement, for example `{ arbitrary::<!>(); }` before the double consume. The call-return-edge fix is not enough there because the enclosing block expression itself remains typed as diverging, so I’m moving one level earlier: erased ghost placeholders of `!` type need statement-position handling that preserves reachability.
codex
The block counterexample points to temporary lifetime handling: `as_temp` already knows a diverging block expression never assigns its temporary and skips `StorageLive`, but it still schedules a value drop for that unassigned `!` temporary. With an erased ghost placeholder now preserving a successor, that scheduled value drop reintroduces an unreachable edge. I’m removing that value drop for the same “never block has no value” case.
