codex
The next change is context-sensitive in MIR building: when an expression statement is exactly Verus’s erased ghost placeholder, I’ll lower only the placeholder’s argument expressions and then continue, instead of materializing a `!` temporary for a value that does not exist at runtime or proof time. This keeps real diverging Rust expressions untouched.
