Root cause/fix:
- Root cause: Verus erases ghost/spec expressions into synthetic `erased_ghost_value` calls. When those synthetic values have uninhabited type, Rust MIR construction treated them like real diverging Rust calls/expressions and removed normal control-flow successors. That made later tracked-resource moves unreachable to borrow/linearity checking.
- Fix: make MIR construction Verus-aware for `erased_ghost_value`: do not remove call successors for synthetic erased ghost values, recognize erased ghost statement placeholders through `Scope`/`NeverToAny`, lower their real subexpressions without treating preserved zero-sized `FnDef` operands as diverging work, reject bare ghost `!` statements with no real subexpressions, and avoid scheduling value drops for temporaries that never receive a value.

